1
00:00:02,230 --> 00:00:08,356
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I wanted to talk about the phenomenon of attraction.

2
00:00:10,458 --> 00:00:13,460
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, the beautiful object in the world.

3
00:00:13,801 --> 00:00:18,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Why are we attracted to that which is beautiful?

4
00:00:20,406 --> 00:00:21,347
[SPEAKER_00]: And I thought about...

5
00:00:23,389 --> 00:00:24,450
[SPEAKER_00]: I came up with an analogy.

6
00:00:27,262 --> 00:00:35,024
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, imagine, well, I'm sure many people experience this when you go into a toilet.

7
00:00:35,344 --> 00:00:44,927
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, I would like visit a friend's house and you go into a toilet after someone has used it and they've sprayed some air freshener.

8
00:00:48,548 --> 00:00:54,130
[SPEAKER_00]: You know why they sprayed it and it highlights that fact.

9
00:00:54,921 --> 00:01:04,108
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no matter what lavender smell is there, for you, you know the underlying smell of feces.

10
00:01:06,150 --> 00:01:08,532
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't kind of ignore it.

11
00:01:08,972 --> 00:01:12,735
[SPEAKER_00]: You can try, obviously, but it's like the person who sprayed it,

12
00:01:14,703 --> 00:01:18,444
[SPEAKER_00]: You kind of can more easily ignore that underlying smell.

13
00:01:18,904 --> 00:01:23,005
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like they focus on that... those fragrances and then they move on.

14
00:01:24,225 --> 00:01:29,027
[SPEAKER_00]: But the next person that comes in, he kind of knows the context of that spray.

15
00:01:29,907 --> 00:01:32,588
[SPEAKER_00]: So... and it highlights that smell underneath.

16
00:01:32,728 --> 00:01:33,048
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

17
00:01:34,688 --> 00:01:38,009
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't... and then the lavender smell is not the same anymore.

18
00:01:38,469 --> 00:01:39,789
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you start associating...

19
00:01:39,829 --> 00:01:41,570
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, you have to buy a different air freshener.

20
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:44,801
[SPEAKER_00]: And so on.

21
00:01:48,663 --> 00:02:04,092
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I think that highlights the fact that, you know, any beautiful object is only... Why are we attracted to a beautiful thing?

22
00:02:05,432 --> 00:02:06,553
[SPEAKER_00]: What makes it so valuable?

23
00:02:08,479 --> 00:02:18,650
[SPEAKER_00]: And I thought that its value is that it tries to, at least, or it does, cover up something, a bad smell.

24
00:02:20,252 --> 00:02:25,278
[SPEAKER_00]: It covers up a larger context of, I'm suffering here.

25
00:02:26,670 --> 00:02:36,313
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I chase something pleasant, I go after the beautiful, because it helps me ignore the the pain that I'm going through.

26
00:02:36,333 --> 00:02:37,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Helps you escape from it, yeah.

27
00:02:37,613 --> 00:02:37,913
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

28
00:02:38,113 --> 00:02:38,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

29
00:02:41,854 --> 00:02:45,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, I think that's the... the Buddha did say that, Sutta.

30
00:02:46,375 --> 00:02:52,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Putujana does not know any other escape from any reason, unpleasant feeling, other than sensuality.

31
00:02:54,498 --> 00:02:56,038
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why... that's why it's like not...

32
00:02:58,480 --> 00:03:00,841
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not as optional as many people might think.

33
00:03:01,181 --> 00:03:03,763
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, you know, I could give this up.

34
00:03:04,403 --> 00:03:12,507
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not necessarily attached to the sense pleasures and stuff, because it feels like, yeah, I can go without things and so on.

35
00:03:13,848 --> 00:03:22,332
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's only true up to a certain extent, because, yes, there is the kind of a preferential add-on to the whole experience of centrality.

36
00:03:23,057 --> 00:03:25,238
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, like a proliferation of preferences and so on.

37
00:03:25,618 --> 00:03:30,259
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's all driven by an underlying existential need to go in that direction.

38
00:03:30,660 --> 00:03:37,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you don't, there is this underlying pressure of dukkha that you are confined with, that you don't know escape from.

39
00:03:38,822 --> 00:03:46,365
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why often a mere idea of giving up sensuality is frightening to people.

40
00:03:47,365 --> 00:03:48,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's when you realize.

41
00:03:48,946 --> 00:03:51,607
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start toying with the idea, that's when you realize

42
00:03:54,904 --> 00:03:57,766
[SPEAKER_01]: How non-optional it really is, your dependence on sensuality.

43
00:03:57,786 --> 00:03:59,908
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a need, you know.

44
00:04:00,549 --> 00:04:06,514
[SPEAKER_01]: You might have options developed on top of that need, but fundamentally it's a need, because it's the only form of escape that you know.

45
00:04:07,615 --> 00:04:15,802
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like for the same reason, basically, people then, generally speaking, it's like

46
00:04:17,833 --> 00:04:46,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, they would try to find a way around it, to not have to do it, because the... just the thought of not having an escape from suffering, the only one that you know, now leaves you really, like, with a... with an acute kind of recognition of that, as I said, confinement, because, oh, the only means of escaping that I knew, I was told they don't work, now what I'm left with is like a complete confinement with

47
00:04:47,283 --> 00:04:51,266
[SPEAKER_01]: With my own existence, dukkha and so on, that... well, I don't know what to do with that.

48
00:04:51,746 --> 00:04:54,768
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's kind of can be quite trapping for the mind and scary.

49
00:04:54,788 --> 00:05:04,554
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why whenever the Buddha was teaching lay people, you know, and they stumbled upon him in conversations, he would give them like a progressive talk.

50
00:05:04,674 --> 00:05:11,278
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, he would be guiding their mind rightly towards, you know, seeing the dhamma and so on.

51
00:05:11,318 --> 00:05:13,320
[SPEAKER_01]: But he would always start with first uplifting it.

52
00:05:15,541 --> 00:05:15,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Because

53
00:05:17,347 --> 00:05:19,068
[SPEAKER_01]: What's about to follow, it's gonna crush it.

54
00:05:20,048 --> 00:05:20,189
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

55
00:05:20,429 --> 00:05:26,952
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if it's uplifted slightly higher, then when it's kind of brought down, it won't be like way too depressed, so to speak.

56
00:05:26,992 --> 00:05:28,193
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be right at the right level.

57
00:05:28,733 --> 00:05:29,813
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, the Buddha could do that.

58
00:05:29,853 --> 00:05:31,834
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, all of the suttas explicitly say that.

59
00:05:32,415 --> 00:05:38,898
[SPEAKER_01]: The Lord saw that their mind is ready, and then he taught them at the right time, to the right extent, guiding their mind.

60
00:05:39,799 --> 00:05:41,920
[SPEAKER_01]: And people can kind of gloss over that sometimes.

61
00:05:41,960 --> 00:05:44,861
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like not a big deal, but it's a huge deal.

62
00:05:45,121 --> 00:05:46,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the fact that

63
00:05:47,294 --> 00:05:56,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Without the Buddha's guidance, you need to meet all those prerequisites and requirements just to be... maybe have a possibility to see the signs of your mind.

64
00:05:57,747 --> 00:06:01,132
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how deeply people do not see their mind for themselves.

65
00:06:02,233 --> 00:06:04,716
[SPEAKER_01]: So you need to be like fully withdrawn from centrality.

66
00:06:06,395 --> 00:06:08,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Then it says, like, you need to be dwelling in seclusion.

67
00:06:08,916 --> 00:06:25,719
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're, like, delighting in company, say, even if you're actually dwelling in seclusion, but internally you still have delight towards mere association with people, talking, seeing people, that will be an obstacle to see the sign of the mind, which is then the obstacle to sotāpati.

68
00:06:26,239 --> 00:06:28,580
[SPEAKER_01]: So, those are prerequisites for the right view.

69
00:06:28,600 --> 00:06:33,761
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, you gotta make... do all the work just to come to the point to see your mind.

70
00:06:35,466 --> 00:06:40,628
[SPEAKER_01]: For that mind to become a reference of, you know, whether to tighten or loosen the strings and all those other symbolism.

71
00:06:41,649 --> 00:06:48,771
[SPEAKER_01]: So, obviously, people that met the Buddha were fortunate for one reason or the other, and he guided their mind without them even seeing their mind.

72
00:06:50,132 --> 00:07:04,318
[SPEAKER_01]: Then they saw it, sure, and when they would exclaim, oh, it's like turning upside... what was upside down, turning it right, what was first place, second, what second place, like correcting the... what was always there, but without his guidance, they wouldn't have been seen.

73
00:07:05,246 --> 00:07:15,373
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the Buddha would talk about the perils of sensuality and so on, it's not about, like, to shame a person or something.

74
00:07:15,633 --> 00:07:17,074
[SPEAKER_01]: It's nothing to do with that.

75
00:07:17,094 --> 00:07:34,787
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just the fact that, if the mind still relies upon sensuality, the entire domain of sensuality, the possibility of sensuality, as means of escaping suffering, deep down, it's never gonna take on that suffering.

76
00:07:35,639 --> 00:07:40,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Because in order to really understand, to correctly understand what the cause of suffering is, you need to take it on.

77
00:07:41,020 --> 00:07:46,022
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just not an option to, oh, I'll avoid it and somehow it's going to get resolved by itself.

78
00:07:47,262 --> 00:07:59,065
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for as long as you're giving in to the ways of avoiding that confinement of the dukkha, seeing the, you know, the four great mountains coming from all directions, as the Buddha would say, recognizing the situation,

79
00:08:00,117 --> 00:08:03,199
[SPEAKER_01]: The opening amidst that confinement will never become apparent.

80
00:08:03,699 --> 00:08:09,062
[SPEAKER_01]: The opening as in the right view, the direction that really takes you out of the whole, frees you from the whole picture.

81
00:08:09,963 --> 00:08:18,368
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about like, oh, you should... you're a bad person or something, if you're still attracted to sense objects and so on.

82
00:08:18,528 --> 00:08:22,771
[SPEAKER_01]: It's way subtler and past any moral judgment.

83
00:08:22,831 --> 00:08:26,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Moral judgments are on the level of five precepts, basically.

84
00:08:26,675 --> 00:08:43,712
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, presumably, if you want to train your mind to the point that you can actually see it correctly, to the point that you can develop cittanimitta, which is a prerequisite for the right view, then it's like, okay, you gotta take these on, these trainings, these confinements, for the sake of kind of

85
00:08:44,597 --> 00:08:55,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, preventing the mind from spilling in the direction where it shouldn't be spilling towards, and then you get to be left with it, the way it is, and with the causes of that suffering right there and then.

86
00:08:55,620 --> 00:09:00,801
[SPEAKER_01]: Not yesterday, not tomorrow, not because of this, not because of that, but because of the state of mind affected with craving.

87
00:09:06,362 --> 00:09:07,523
[SPEAKER_00]: So, also that...

88
00:09:11,236 --> 00:09:15,963
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are attracted to a beautiful thing, you're doing it.

89
00:09:16,024 --> 00:09:16,805
[SPEAKER_00]: You are attracted.

90
00:09:16,825 --> 00:09:17,706
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a reason for that.

91
00:09:17,987 --> 00:09:20,691
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a reason that is attractive for you.

92
00:09:22,173 --> 00:09:24,196
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason is you are...

93
00:09:25,143 --> 00:09:30,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Uncomfortable here, you're suffering here, you can't face it, you're trying... looking for an escape from suffering.

94
00:09:30,546 --> 00:09:40,512
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you... Well, it doesn't... not necessarily... it doesn't need to be like that kind of relational, in a sense, that whenever you're attracted, it's because you're suffering right there and then, and you're escaping something.

95
00:09:41,813 --> 00:09:46,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, people can genuinely be fine, overall, but still cultivate the attraction.

96
00:09:47,996 --> 00:09:52,699
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's nothing currently there undermining them, in terms of suffering, in terms of a threat.

97
00:09:54,999 --> 00:09:56,041
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

98
00:09:56,322 --> 00:10:01,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the way you said it, it sounds like whenever you're attracted, it's because something is right there you're trying to escape from.

99
00:10:03,616 --> 00:10:07,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it might not be an acute suffering, but in general it's a...

100
00:10:08,883 --> 00:10:11,864
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an escape from the liability to suffering, you know.

101
00:10:12,265 --> 00:10:13,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yes, yeah.

102
00:10:13,865 --> 00:10:14,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

103
00:10:14,285 --> 00:10:20,668
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like a subtle thing and like... well, even just an escape from discontent.

104
00:10:21,369 --> 00:10:21,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

105
00:10:21,669 --> 00:10:22,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Boredom.

106
00:10:22,409 --> 00:10:23,550
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's right.

107
00:10:23,690 --> 00:10:31,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Those things are already these subtle unpleasantness that if you don't escape from, would turn into full-on pressure and anxiety and panic attack.

108
00:10:31,433 --> 00:10:37,356
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like the neutrality of my present experience is just too much

109
00:10:38,276 --> 00:10:39,937
[SPEAKER_00]: For the mind to handle, it's like...

110
00:10:40,778 --> 00:10:45,300
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's by chasing something beautiful, that is ignored.

111
00:10:45,320 --> 00:10:46,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's also the chase as well.

112
00:10:47,241 --> 00:11:03,011
[SPEAKER_01]: The chase is the... Having that chase, having that movement, having that activation, pretty much, that's exactly, kind of, already offers... See, the fact that you could move, already implies that you're not as confined as you might have felt.

113
00:11:03,711 --> 00:11:07,794
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, fundamentally, saṅkharas follow immediately after avijja.

114
00:11:08,993 --> 00:11:12,860
[SPEAKER_01]: The movement follows immediately after ignorance and it proliferates that ignorance.

115
00:11:17,768 --> 00:11:19,551
[SPEAKER_00]: But, I guess, interesting point.

116
00:11:22,297 --> 00:11:24,517
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, so, just thinking about the chase.

117
00:11:25,078 --> 00:11:45,542
[SPEAKER_00]: So, chasing some attractive object or beautiful object, chasing it, you know, helps with ignoring of the greater context, perpetuating, which doesn't change that thing in the background, that you are the reason for your chase.

118
00:11:46,722 --> 00:11:52,123
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when you get to that, when you get that pleasant thing that you're chasing, whatever it might be,

119
00:11:54,953 --> 00:11:56,654
[SPEAKER_00]: That background context remains there.

120
00:11:57,195 --> 00:11:58,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, nothing has changed, really.

121
00:11:58,496 --> 00:12:00,057
[SPEAKER_00]: But now the chase has stopped.

122
00:12:00,577 --> 00:12:00,837
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

123
00:12:01,338 --> 00:12:02,378
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you've got to find a new one.

124
00:12:03,819 --> 00:12:06,201
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, you're running out of options.

125
00:12:06,741 --> 00:12:09,563
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you have to keep on diversifying your chases.

126
00:12:09,583 --> 00:12:24,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, you're running out of options and also your senses, your sense organs are aging, disintegrating, which means sights will be harder to see, sounds will be harder to enjoy,

127
00:12:24,832 --> 00:12:27,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Taste won't offer as much of a relief as before.

128
00:12:27,654 --> 00:12:36,278
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you won't be able to to be distracted on account of sense objects, because your senses cannot appreciate them as much as before.

129
00:12:36,318 --> 00:12:40,901
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just aching, failing, not as good as it was, and so on.

130
00:12:41,201 --> 00:12:45,003
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's also like an impending thing in the direction where you're heading.

131
00:12:48,365 --> 00:12:52,147
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, if you are noticing or you understand that

132
00:12:53,633 --> 00:13:07,944
[SPEAKER_00]: I would say, if you understand that a thing is attractive, and it's that the beautiful, the value in that beautiful thing, is to chase, because it covers up your discontent now.

133
00:13:08,865 --> 00:13:09,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

134
00:13:09,325 --> 00:13:17,772
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't chase that thing, and you understand that you are actually discontent here and now, that's why you... That's why you'll be going...

135
00:13:17,812 --> 00:13:18,272
[SPEAKER_00]: Attracted.

136
00:13:18,312 --> 00:13:18,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

137
00:13:20,546 --> 00:13:27,042
[SPEAKER_00]: And that getting that thing doesn't actually resolve the issue of your resistance to this discontent here.

138
00:13:28,802 --> 00:13:30,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it does offer a relief, though.

139
00:13:30,223 --> 00:13:31,263
[SPEAKER_00]: It offers a relief, but not...

140
00:13:31,283 --> 00:13:33,724
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not just like, oh, it's... nothing is real, it's not true.

141
00:13:33,784 --> 00:13:34,284
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

142
00:13:34,404 --> 00:13:36,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality offers a factual relief.

143
00:13:36,726 --> 00:13:39,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Chase offers a factual relief through distraction.

144
00:13:39,427 --> 00:13:39,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

145
00:13:40,007 --> 00:13:47,450
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just that you need to recognize and convince yourself rightly that those reliefs are actually making things worse.

146
00:13:47,590 --> 00:13:49,131
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not truly relieving you.

147
00:13:49,851 --> 00:13:57,835
[SPEAKER_01]: They're giving you a temporary kind of cover-up, but at the expense of making the whole condition worse, like scratching the...

148
00:13:58,559 --> 00:14:02,524
[SPEAKER_01]: The itch relieves you, factually relieves you, factually makes you feel, ah, now it's better.

149
00:14:02,544 --> 00:14:08,892
[SPEAKER_01]: The itch is gone, but it's making the whole skin condition a lot, a lot worse, so it's gonna itch even harder next time.

150
00:14:10,364 --> 00:14:13,445
[SPEAKER_01]: And the time will come when you won't be able to scratch, basically, one way or the other.

151
00:14:13,725 --> 00:14:17,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Either because your hands don't work or because there's no more things to scratch with.

152
00:14:18,686 --> 00:14:27,029
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, that highlights the the danger of the chasing and also the escape.

153
00:14:27,969 --> 00:14:31,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, like, well, that's the... this is the perpetuation of my problem.

154
00:14:31,310 --> 00:14:33,031
[SPEAKER_01]: What highlights the danger in the escape?

155
00:14:33,991 --> 00:14:38,032
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowing that, what you just said, you know, that... And what highlights the knowing of that?

156
00:14:40,845 --> 00:14:41,626
[SPEAKER_00]: What highlights it?

157
00:14:41,886 --> 00:14:42,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what brings it about?

158
00:14:44,869 --> 00:14:46,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Self-reflection.

159
00:14:46,430 --> 00:14:47,891
[SPEAKER_01]: And what brings that about?

160
00:14:49,212 --> 00:14:49,533
[SPEAKER_00]: Dukkha.

161
00:14:49,893 --> 00:14:50,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering.

162
00:14:51,094 --> 00:14:51,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

163
00:14:51,494 --> 00:14:56,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering that you basically impose by first and foremost saying no to those things.

164
00:14:56,699 --> 00:14:57,720
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you heard about it.

165
00:14:57,940 --> 00:15:04,146
[SPEAKER_01]: You could think and ponder all you want and analyze and and and and psychologize and make all the connections.

166
00:15:04,266 --> 00:15:06,528
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't give it up first,

167
00:15:08,897 --> 00:15:10,759
[SPEAKER_01]: None of this will have anything to land on.

168
00:15:12,120 --> 00:15:19,647
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's because sometimes people would think, well, I know I should give it up, and if I ponder hard enough, it will be given up for me, so to speak.

169
00:15:20,287 --> 00:15:28,735
[SPEAKER_01]: If I meditate rightly, or if I do this rightly, then I'll just lose interest in centrality altogether, without having to just give it up out of faith first.

170
00:15:30,068 --> 00:15:30,969
[SPEAKER_01]: And that will never work.

171
00:15:31,549 --> 00:15:42,735
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why even when the when the Arahants wanted, after the Buddha passed away, when they were deciding what to chant first, what to recite first, dhamma or the vinaya, they said, well, there is no dhamma without vinaya.

172
00:15:43,435 --> 00:15:52,360
[SPEAKER_01]: So they spent, I don't know how long, reciting just absolute technicalities of what you shouldn't do, and if you do that, what are the conditions, the whole patimokkha, basically.

173
00:15:53,309 --> 00:16:00,792
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, even, even mindlessly, like, as in, like, I have no... any understanding of the right view, the right practice.

174
00:16:02,473 --> 00:16:03,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

175
00:16:04,933 --> 00:16:07,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality needs to be said no to first.

176
00:16:07,935 --> 00:16:10,115
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to be restrained first.

177
00:16:10,155 --> 00:16:11,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can develop the understanding.

178
00:16:11,416 --> 00:16:14,157
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, it's... you could do both at the same time.

179
00:16:14,577 --> 00:16:16,458
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you cannot do is not say no,

180
00:16:17,316 --> 00:16:25,581
[SPEAKER_01]: to sensuality as means of existential escape that we just described, and think that pondering about dhamma will somehow do it for you.

181
00:16:26,422 --> 00:16:33,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Pondering about dhamma without saying no might make you... help you fortify the reasons for eventually saying no to it.

182
00:16:34,447 --> 00:16:40,391
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's only after you say no to it that then you can know, okay, now I am heading in the right direction.

183
00:16:41,355 --> 00:16:50,621
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if I'm still not terribly clear about the nuances of those directions, the fact that the training has been undertaken, means I am heading in the right direction.

184
00:16:50,641 --> 00:16:52,002
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why vinaya means training.

185
00:16:52,663 --> 00:16:59,327
[SPEAKER_01]: Monks' rules, usually, vinaya basically means monks' rules, what they shouldn't do, means training.

186
00:16:59,508 --> 00:17:02,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Since when one undertakes the vinaya, means one undertakes the training of the dhamma.

187
00:17:05,298 --> 00:17:08,300
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because it reveals that discontent.

188
00:17:08,561 --> 00:17:09,321
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

189
00:17:09,661 --> 00:17:14,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't necessarily see it or understand it at the time, it brings it up there.

190
00:17:14,765 --> 00:17:15,646
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't cover it up.

191
00:17:16,266 --> 00:17:21,010
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eventually, you'll have stuff to work with on the right level, because it always has been there.

192
00:17:21,030 --> 00:17:29,877
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just that you've been, like, too busy immediately, automatically covering it up and just following the directions of distraction and so on.

193
00:17:38,680 --> 00:17:47,965
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, the escape... so, you don't... you say no, the danger becomes clear, the escape becomes clearer.

194
00:17:47,985 --> 00:17:48,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, eventually.

195
00:17:48,705 --> 00:17:49,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, things become clearer.

196
00:17:50,726 --> 00:17:53,108
[SPEAKER_01]: You say no, and then the confinement becomes clear.

197
00:17:53,188 --> 00:18:00,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Confinement, yeah, and... Because now you're left with, like, with just an intense pressure to just, well, yeah, run, go back to the wilderness.

198
00:18:00,772 --> 00:18:00,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

199
00:18:01,092 --> 00:18:02,412
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the elephant has been caught.

200
00:18:03,673 --> 00:18:12,924
[SPEAKER_01]: For for at least a certain amount of time, all that is going to think about is its wilderness that has been extracted from.

201
00:18:13,885 --> 00:18:21,474
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, when you take on the restraint, training in restraint, yeah, initially, all the mind will think about is things you said no to.

202
00:18:21,975 --> 00:18:23,457
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to feel worse in that sense.

203
00:18:24,371 --> 00:18:29,612
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't open the gates, if you don't lower the fences, then it will come down within that.

204
00:18:29,693 --> 00:18:36,534
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you get to see that the whole urge towards that wilderness was the reason you were going into the wilderness.

205
00:18:37,375 --> 00:18:52,539
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, if the arising of sensuality in your mind, sensual desire, that's the word I was looking for, if the arising of desire in your mind was not already, in and of itself, unpleasant,

206
00:18:54,530 --> 00:19:01,653
[SPEAKER_01]: You would have never gone to the lengths and pursuits of sense objects as you used to do, as you would be doing.

207
00:19:02,554 --> 00:19:06,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if the arising of desire is neutral, well, it ends there and then.

208
00:19:07,676 --> 00:19:10,277
[SPEAKER_01]: But the arising of desire already hurts.

209
00:19:11,818 --> 00:19:16,860
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean, like, restraint reveals that sensuality is actually painful.

210
00:19:17,845 --> 00:19:26,929
[SPEAKER_01]: But unless you're restrained, whenever sensuality arises with that pain, you project it onto the sense objects, like, oh, it hurts because I don't have that.

211
00:19:27,609 --> 00:19:29,990
[SPEAKER_01]: So then you go for it to appease the pain.

212
00:19:30,791 --> 00:19:32,591
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact is, it still hurts first.

213
00:19:34,112 --> 00:19:36,253
[SPEAKER_01]: So the sensuality is factually unpleasant.

214
00:19:36,673 --> 00:19:37,954
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense desire is factually unpleasant.

215
00:19:37,974 --> 00:19:40,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter the amount of pleasure you gain on top of it.

216
00:19:42,492 --> 00:19:45,274
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always going to be driven by the pain of the desire.

217
00:19:45,454 --> 00:19:48,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if desire were not painful, well, you wouldn't be moving with it.

218
00:19:48,196 --> 00:19:48,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

219
00:19:48,717 --> 00:19:50,118
[SPEAKER_00]: There would be no activity.

220
00:19:50,318 --> 00:19:50,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

221
00:19:52,099 --> 00:19:53,020
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't be a desire.

222
00:19:53,420 --> 00:19:59,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you even conceive any desire that's not slightly uncomfortable?

223
00:19:59,524 --> 00:20:05,849
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't carry that thread of discomfort that you're trying to basically escape, run away from.

224
00:20:06,309 --> 00:20:06,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Appease.

225
00:20:09,792 --> 00:20:10,212
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh...

226
00:20:13,325 --> 00:20:26,837
[SPEAKER_00]: So, seeing that and not, no longer resisting the discomfort, then what value has beauty?

227
00:20:27,758 --> 00:20:39,829
[SPEAKER_00]: And what, what is the problem with, you know, you're no longer, if you're no longer resisting your discontent, your dukkha here, then

228
00:20:42,040 --> 00:20:43,682
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no need for a beautiful thing.

229
00:20:43,762 --> 00:20:47,565
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, eventually, the whole, yeah, the whole beautiful things are seen for what they are.

230
00:20:48,446 --> 00:20:51,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Escape from the pain that they inflicted upon you in the first place.

231
00:20:53,892 --> 00:20:57,295
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a vicious, it's a vicious cycle, exactly like a, like an addiction.

232
00:20:58,216 --> 00:21:00,598
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason you go back to drugs is because you've been taking them.

233
00:21:02,200 --> 00:21:04,442
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you need to go back to them, because you've been taking them.

234
00:21:05,696 --> 00:21:08,658
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop taking them, you won't be going back to them.

235
00:21:09,478 --> 00:21:13,340
[SPEAKER_01]: But time has to pass until the mind calms down away from it.

236
00:21:14,821 --> 00:21:17,522
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is no other way to go about it.

237
00:21:18,742 --> 00:21:24,045
[SPEAKER_01]: How much you're gonna suffer on account of it, depends how wild your mind was to begin with.

238
00:21:25,145 --> 00:21:34,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people's minds were not that wild, and when they impose the level of that kind of an infinite notion of I will never go back to these things,

239
00:21:35,697 --> 00:21:37,679
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind panics a little bit, but it calms down.

240
00:21:37,739 --> 00:21:43,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people, it just goes into full-on panic attack, like, like, too hard to bear, but... You have to go through it, you know.

241
00:21:43,543 --> 00:21:54,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you have to go through it, like, and if it's too much, again, you do it gradually, you'll fail, you go back to it, but at no point should you allow yourself to develop views that, I don't have to go through it.

242
00:21:57,142 --> 00:22:13,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Because even if you just put everything else aside from the suttas, just take the sheer amount of similes that the Buddha gave about taming the elephant or the thoroughbred horse and the colts and stuff, and just think about the principles of training a wild animal, and you'll see it always boils down to that, and there is no way around it.

243
00:22:14,012 --> 00:22:14,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

244
00:22:14,912 --> 00:22:16,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Like... Yeah.

245
00:22:16,574 --> 00:22:27,812
[SPEAKER_01]: The elephant, used to the wilderness, roaming where he wants, doing what he wants, will resist, kick and scream, urinate and defecate, as the suttas say, until he calms down when being trapped.

246
00:22:28,735 --> 00:22:30,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, when it comes down, that's okay now.

247
00:22:31,016 --> 00:22:34,458
[SPEAKER_01]: We can begin to, like, offer it food, take it for a walk.

248
00:22:34,818 --> 00:22:36,179
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you could begin to train it.

249
00:22:36,939 --> 00:22:45,944
[SPEAKER_01]: But trying to train, trying to command and tell elephant what to do, while it's still allowed to roam in the wilderness, well, that's just like talking to a... to a rock or something.

250
00:22:45,964 --> 00:22:46,704
[SPEAKER_01]: It makes no difference.

251
00:22:46,744 --> 00:22:48,105
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no communication there.

252
00:22:52,827 --> 00:22:54,208
[SPEAKER_00]: So, would you... would you say, then,

253
00:22:56,155 --> 00:23:11,742
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I think one thing to add would be, like, you know, if a person hears this, it's like, yes, there's an imperative you must abandon sensuality, but it's not something that you must, or you even can, do it overnight.

254
00:23:11,922 --> 00:23:16,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, okay, from tomorrow onwards, never again, and then I'll be a sotāpanna.

255
00:23:17,004 --> 00:23:21,806
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to recognize, well, you could try, from tomorrow onwards, never again, and then see what happens tomorrow.

256
00:23:23,025 --> 00:23:35,732
[SPEAKER_01]: See, when your mind gets... when that elephant wakes up and wants to go back to the... what it's used to, the pastures and stuff, see what force comes up, how forceful it is, most likely you're gonna fail.

257
00:23:37,092 --> 00:23:37,853
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the point.

258
00:23:37,933 --> 00:23:42,675
[SPEAKER_01]: To recognize that it's not about, like, as I said, a moral commandment, it's about training.

259
00:23:45,457 --> 00:23:51,040
[SPEAKER_01]: So, five precepts, add celibacy, add more precepts gradually, but it's all about the training.

260
00:23:51,080 --> 00:23:51,480
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about

261
00:23:53,082 --> 00:24:00,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Preventing that mind from spilling into these unwholesome wildernesses and ways of basically existing, they were covering up.

262
00:24:01,598 --> 00:24:09,242
[SPEAKER_01]: They were covering up the dukkha, covering up the source of dukkha, covering up discomfort, and just keeping it dependent on that constant roaming about, emotional.

263
00:24:09,342 --> 00:24:10,683
[SPEAKER_01]: I want this, I want that, I want this.

264
00:24:11,063 --> 00:24:15,346
[SPEAKER_01]: And if there is nothing left for me to do or go, then the panic sets in and anxiety comes up and stuff.

265
00:24:15,366 --> 00:24:24,911
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, you want to address that before that actually comes, before the elephant is too old to move and stuff, but the mind still wants to go and do all of these things.

266
00:24:25,632 --> 00:24:30,054
[SPEAKER_01]: And then now you're just being, well, like the old king in that sutta, when he would have said, well, I'm

267
00:24:32,020 --> 00:24:35,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he asked him, like, what would you do if I tell you there's a big army coming from the east?

268
00:24:36,283 --> 00:24:37,844
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, well, I'll go and fight them there.

269
00:24:37,904 --> 00:24:39,205
[SPEAKER_01]: From the west, I'll go and fight them there.

270
00:24:39,565 --> 00:24:43,147
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I tell you they're, like, coming from all directions?

271
00:24:43,167 --> 00:24:44,348
[SPEAKER_01]: No, sorry, no, it wasn't the big army.

272
00:24:44,448 --> 00:24:47,190
[SPEAKER_01]: It was the... What if I tell you there's a big mountain coming from the east?

273
00:24:47,210 --> 00:24:49,671
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, crushing all things... Crushing all things in its way, yeah.

274
00:24:49,731 --> 00:24:51,172
[SPEAKER_01]: And he says, well, I would run to the west.

275
00:24:51,232 --> 00:24:52,933
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I tell you there's a mountain coming from the west?

276
00:24:52,993 --> 00:24:53,174
[SPEAKER_01]: No,

277
00:25:03,020 --> 00:25:04,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's actually heading towards the destruction.

278
00:25:04,941 --> 00:25:05,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

279
00:25:05,741 --> 00:25:10,803
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, he says, well, in that case, I would give up the lay life and practice the dhamma, well, because the time is short.

280
00:25:13,665 --> 00:25:20,848
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like... so, that... that recognition, that... that... that principle, that framework, that... there is no... as I said, there's no changing that.

281
00:25:21,888 --> 00:25:26,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can recognize your current limitations and then start working on your environment.

282
00:25:27,070 --> 00:25:27,911
[SPEAKER_01]: They would then support

283
00:25:29,205 --> 00:25:36,649
[SPEAKER_01]: you training in the precepts more, they would then, obviously, calm down the mind gradually, more and more and more.

284
00:25:37,249 --> 00:25:49,154
[SPEAKER_01]: So, don't expect, like, to just magically resolve, because you have such a strong determination, and take it over, like, next day, take on all the precepts, and sense restraint, and then the mind will just magically follow.

285
00:25:49,194 --> 00:25:49,334
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

286
00:25:49,374 --> 00:25:49,875
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to be

287
00:25:50,675 --> 00:25:54,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Ups and downs, but the most important thing is that your view and the value of doing it doesn't change.

288
00:25:55,299 --> 00:26:01,805
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't, halfway through, convince yourself, no, I don't actually have to keep the priest, no, I don't actually have to... No, you don't need to give these things up.

289
00:26:02,425 --> 00:26:04,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, deep down, you know that you do.

290
00:26:04,607 --> 00:26:11,793
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, you don't want to believe it, because it's too uncomfortable, and you don't want training to be about going through that discomfort.

291
00:26:11,813 --> 00:26:13,254
[SPEAKER_01]: You want it to be about something else.

292
00:26:13,514 --> 00:26:16,397
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's just now... becomes delusional, basically.

293
00:26:23,233 --> 00:26:43,556
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as I was saying, like in the beginning, that the fact that you are attracted, you understand all these things to some degree, the fact that you are attracted reveals that there is a problem, that the mind is still... there is some resistance right here to something.

294
00:26:43,876 --> 00:26:44,497
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

295
00:26:45,237 --> 00:26:55,179
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, at that kind of stage, so to speak, or whatever, attraction reveals the issue.

296
00:26:55,439 --> 00:26:57,139
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a kind of revealing issue.

297
00:26:57,699 --> 00:26:59,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, saying no to attraction would reveal the issue.

298
00:26:59,420 --> 00:27:01,380
[SPEAKER_01]: Saying no to attraction, I'm not... Because if you're still going with it...

299
00:27:01,460 --> 00:27:03,561
[SPEAKER_00]: I know I'm attracted, but I'm not going towards it.

300
00:27:03,681 --> 00:27:03,861
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

301
00:27:04,061 --> 00:27:07,741
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if I go towards it, of course, yeah, then I'm ignoring the...

302
00:27:08,082 --> 00:27:08,382
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm trying...

303
00:27:08,422 --> 00:27:09,242
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm covering things up.

304
00:27:09,382 --> 00:27:09,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

305
00:27:10,802 --> 00:27:21,369
[SPEAKER_00]: But knowing attraction is there, and not saying no to it, it highlights the fact why it's there, why it has such a pull.

306
00:27:21,749 --> 00:27:30,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there's a push here, away from just boredom, neutrality, or pain, or whatever.

307
00:27:30,214 --> 00:27:30,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Confinement.

308
00:27:30,935 --> 00:27:32,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, confinement.

309
00:27:32,996 --> 00:27:33,336
[SPEAKER_00]: Dukkha.

310
00:27:34,197 --> 00:27:35,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is dukkha, already form of dukkha, yeah?

311
00:27:38,960 --> 00:27:49,423
[SPEAKER_00]: And that, as that sutta, one sutta says, that if you stop delighting in... you get rid of the light, that craving, then beautiful things in the world just remain as they are.

312
00:27:49,583 --> 00:27:49,863
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

313
00:27:50,003 --> 00:27:50,944
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like...

314
00:27:50,964 --> 00:27:54,325
[SPEAKER_01]: The things haven't changed, but you just completely overcome your desire towards them.

315
00:27:54,345 --> 00:28:02,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you stop running out from confinement, that... that patiga of your own existence, you stop running away from it.

316
00:28:02,147 --> 00:28:04,828
[SPEAKER_01]: And you stop running away from it, the purpose

317
00:28:06,274 --> 00:28:11,779
[SPEAKER_01]: Depending on all these beautiful things and using them for distraction, that completely evaporates.

318
00:28:11,799 --> 00:28:12,879
[SPEAKER_01]: The value is gone.

319
00:28:12,920 --> 00:28:13,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

320
00:28:14,060 --> 00:28:16,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the reasons for engaging with sensuality are gone now.

321
00:28:19,685 --> 00:28:25,589
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's no need for movement of the sensual kind through the desire.

